  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1139 AM CDT Sun Oct 18 2026\n\nTonight\n\nWith the loss of diurnal heating and the surface pressure\ngradient decreasing, the winds will quickly subside tonight.\nWith light winds and dry dew points, temperatures quickly cool\novernight. The NBM looked to be a bit too warm, so did a 50-50\nblend of the NBM and the NBM 10th percentile. This lowered\ntemperatures up to 5F. The largest drop was in central and\nnorth-central Wisconsin. Low temperatures tonight will range\nfrom the lower 30s to mid-40s.\n\nMonday - Friday\n\nA 500 mb ridge will gradually build north into the region early\nthis week and then keep the area dry through Friday. High \ntemperatures will be mainly in the 60s through Wednesday and \nthen range from the upper 60s to mid-70s for the remainder of \nthe week. Low temperatures will range from the mid-30s to \nmid-40s on Monday and Tuesday nights, and in the 40s and 50s for\nthe remainder of the week. \n\nNext Weekend\n\nA negatively-tilted northern stream shortwave will lift\nnortheast from the Central and Northern Plains into Ontario. In\nits wake, the upper-level ridge will build back across the \narea. This trough will provide a chance of rain from Saturday\nafternoon into Sunday morning. With dry air across the region, \nthe rain amounts will be on light side (generally less than a \ntrace). High temperatures will be in the 60s and lower 70s. Low \ntemperatures will range from the upper 40s to mid-50s.",
